> think this is the way many sees it, without saying it clearly.
>
> the additional cost of not doing 'eb' is quite moderate, so there is
> less to gain computationally these days
>
> if the uncertaintay is small, there is little difference, which can
> justufy using 'eb' but there are no formal justifcation in general
>
> H
>
>
> On Tue, 2023-08-29 at 03:04 -0700, Sébastien Coube wrote:
> > Hello,
> > Do you have some insight on how much we lose in terms of prediction
> > and estimation when using an Empirical Bayes approach instead of full
> > INLA/MCMC/whatever in a geostatistical model ?
> > I think the answer to be "yes" with data sets that give some
> > significant freedom to the posterior distribution for the
> > SPDE/covariance parameters. Håvard and L Held's disease mapping papers
> > identified and addressed the issue of the coupling of the latent field
> > and the high-level parameters. Do you confirm this lead ?
> > However, with large data sets that should give very slim posterior
> > distributions, do you think that there is a need for full exploration
> > in order to predict and estimate correctly the spatial effects and the
> > effects of covariates ?
> > Thanks in advance
> > Sébastien
